{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: give each step of the working cycle its own gs command, and let gs work tell an actor its next act.\n\nWhy. The discipline is stated in prose across SKILL.md (5,200 words), AGENTS.md, 28 reference pages and 42 notes, and an actor reconstructs every act's shape by hand: full event ids, which events to rest on, which artifact to name first, publish order, body fields, idempotency keys. Three in ten tool calls in local agent sessions touch a rule document; my own memory holds 202 workaround notes for this workroom. The two commands that already encode a whole step, gs review and gs merge, are the ones with the fewest refiles. Extend that to the rest of the cycle.\n\nOutcome. Four commands, each preflighting the fold's preconditions before signing and refusing with a named fix: gs promise \u003crequest\u003e (rests on the request; refuses if the request is not addressed to the actor, is not open, or the actor already holds a live promise on it; warns if the request is stale); gs artifact --head \u003csha\u003e --promise \u003cpromise\u003e [--branch] \u003cpath…\u003e (publishes one artifact per path at that head resting on exactly that promise, the reporting artifact last; refuses a path not changed against the request's target, warns about changed paths not listed; accepts extra --rests-on for documentation pages that describe behaviour); gs review-request --head \u003csha\u003e --to \u003cactor\u003e [--text] (rests on every live artifact of the actor at that head, names the reporting artifact, sets head, branch, no_git_artifact and default conditions; refuses when artifacts stand at different heads or when the actor already has a live review request for that promise unless --replace, which retires it; warns when the governing request is stale); gs land --approval \u003cevent\u003e --checkout \u003cpath\u003e --text \u003ctext\u003e [--cleanup] (ratifies the approval when the actor is its review requester and it is unratified, runs the merge plan, runs the merge, retries once on a moved frontier, pushes the target ref to origin, and with --cleanup removes the candidate worktree and branch only after the head is an ancestor of the target). And gs work --next, which prints for each row the actor owes the next required act as an exact gs command line, including open unclaimed requests addressed to the actor, stale rows, and asserts resting on the actor's promises. Each command is composed from the existing state, batch, ratify, merge-plan and merge paths; no new fold rule, kind or field.","body":{"branch":"request/step-commands","conditions":"1. gs promise, gs artifact, gs review-request and gs land exist with the behaviour and refusals stated in the request text, composed from existing act paths, and every refusal names the fix. 2. gs work --next prints one exact command line per owed act, covering open unclaimed requests addressed to the actor, promised rows, awaiting-review and awaiting-landing rows, stale rows, and asserts resting on the actor's promises; a row with nothing to do says why. 3. Each command has tests for its accepted path and each refusal, asserting nothing was appended on refusal; gs land is tested end to end including --cleanup gating on ancestry. 4. A reference page per command under docs/reference/gs/ with a runnable example, and docs/reference/gs/work.md updated. 5. go test ./... and the docset gate green at the exact head; independent review by codex; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"c3cc0e4479f9d0e3a5f04e20ecdcfecdc492ecd9","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}